<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bhanja dynasty</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bhanja dynasty is a ruling family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, known from early medieval inscriptions and claiming ancient descent. Branches ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> of Mayurbhanj and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kendujhar">Keonjhar</a>, and related lines held other small states in Odisha, until these merged into the Indian Union in 1948–1949.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bhanja-dynasty.jpg" alt="Bhanja dynasty" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Takshashila7977 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Bengal_in_958_CE.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Bhanjas arose in the north and centre of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, in the Khiching region and the Khinjali mandala, and the historian Hermann Kulke described their lineage as ancient local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshatriya">Kshatriya</a>. They succeeded the Vindhyatabi branch of the Nagas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pawaya">Padmavati</a>, who ruled from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kendujhar">Keonjhar</a> district, and later became feudatories of the Bhauma-Kara dynasty, governing territories called mandalas. Their early inscriptions trace their origin to a mythical peahen, and peafowl appear on the emblems shared by the later branches.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Khinjali mandala covered the areas of present-day Boudh, Phulbani, Nayagarh, Ghumusar and Sonepur, with its capital at Dhritipura, modern Boudh. Copper plate inscriptions show Bhanja rule there continuing until the 12th century. In the early 10th century two Khinjali princesses, Vakula Mahadevi and Dharma Mahadevi, married the Bhauma-Kara kings Shantikaradeva III and Shubhakaradeva V and later reigned over the Bhauma-Kara kingdom themselves, before it passed to the Somavamshis.</p><p>The Khijjinga mandala, covering present-day Mayurbhanj and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kendujhar">Kendujhar</a>, was ruled from Khijjinga Kota, now Khiching, by a line its inscriptions call the Adi-Bhanja dynasty, founded by Virabhadra Adi-Bhanja in the 8th century. Its early history is known from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> plates found at Bamanghaty near Rairangpur, among them an inscription of Ranabhanja of 924. The line declined under the Somavamshis and rose again as feudatories of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-ganga-dynasty">Eastern Ganga dynasty</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Many later houses traced their descent from the Bhanjas. Mayurbhanj State is generally held to descend from Adi Bhanja, and the tradition of Keonjhar State names Jyoti Bhanj as its first raja, enthroned with the help of the local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhuyan">Bhuyan</a> clans. Other branches ruled Baudh from the 14th century, Daspalla from about 1498 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindari</a> of Ghumusar, whose rulers, among them Kabi Samrat Upendra Bhanja, shaped Odia literature in the period called the Bhanja Yuga. The British annexed Ghumusar after the rebellion of 1835-36.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bhanja dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tradition holds that the ruling family was a branch of the <b>Bhanja</b> rulers of Baudh State, founding Daspalla around 1498.</blockquote><a href="/state/daspalla-state">Daspalla State</a></li><li><blockquote>Tradition holds that Keonjhar was founded in the 12th century by a younger brother of the ruler of Mayurbhanj, so that both states were ruled by branches of the <b>Bhanja dynasty</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/keonjhar-state">Keonjhar State</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Bhanja dynasty</b> of Mayurbhanj claimed ancient descent, and tradition places its rule from around 1100, first at Haripur and later at Baripada.</blockquote><a href="/state/mayurbhanj-state">Mayurbhanj State</a></li><li><blockquote>The ruling family claimed descent from the <b>Bhanja</b> rulers of the region, and tradition dates the state to the early 16th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/nilgiri-state">Nilgiri State</a></li></ul></section>
